{"language_code":"eng","language_probability":0.9331080913543701,"text":"Okay, so every coding interview eventually hits you with the same question: What's the Big O of this? And honestly, you can read it right off the code. Let me show you. Big O isn't about seconds or how fast your laptop is. It's really just one question. If the input doubles, what happens to the work? Let's start with the easy one, O of one. A hash lookup just jumps straight to the answer. Ten items, ten million, doesn't matter. It's pretty much one step. Next up, O of n. That's a single loop touching each item once. So if you double the input, yep, you double the work. And here's the one that gets people, O of n squared. It's a loop inside a loop, so every item gets checked against every other item. Eight numbers, twenty-eight checks. A million numbers, we're talking about five hundred billion. So when n doubles, O of one stays the same. O of n doubles, and O of n squared, four times the work. We actually timed it and, yeah, that's exactly what happened. So next time you spot a loop inside a loop in an interview, call it out. That's O of n squared. Then aim for O of n, usually with a HashMap. Save this one and learn the rest on Leet Expert.","words":[{"text":"Okay,","start":0.06,"end":0.4,"type":"word","logprob":-0.01407776708365418},{"text":" ","start":0.4,"end":0.62,"type":"spacing","logprob":-3.576278118089249e-7},{"text":"so","start":0.62,"end":0.86,"type":"word","logprob":-3.576278118089249e-7},{"text":" ","start":0.86,"end":0.94,"type":"spacing","logprob":-5.960462772236497e-7},{"text":"every","start":0.94,"end":1.22,"type":"word","logprob":-5.960462772236497e-7},{"text":" ","start":1.22,"end":1.26,"type":"spacing","logprob":-0.00001680836794548668},{"text":"coding","start":1.26,"end":1.54,"type":"word","logprob":-0.00001680836794548668},{"text":" ","start":1.54,"end":1.58,"type":"spacing","logprob":-2.145764938177308e-6},{"text":"interview","start":1.58,"end":2.04,"type":"word","logprob"